A||||1||eggman||Electrical leak while car is off?||||1||
Z||000000||eggman||04-25-2000||01:07 PM||||Here's a strange one.  If I don't drive my '86 GT for more than a couple of days, the battery goes dead.  I've checked the system with the car off using an ammeter in series with the battery, and there is virtually no current flow  (my Toyota has a lot more current flowing with the car "off", and can go for weeks without operation -- then fire right up).<P>The alternator is fine, since the car can run perfectly without the battery and the battery charges right up once I jump start the car.  I've replaced the battery, but this still happens.<P>Any suggestions?<P>Thanks,<P>Eggman ||||reg||1
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||04-25-2000||02:57 PM||||This happens a lot to people with GTs. Why? Because GTs are the one most likely to have a trunk light. A tolerance space is opened up after a while and the light sometimes doesn't go off when you close the trunk. Drains the battery after a while. A way you can check if its this is to check the voltage with the trunk light off, then on. Keep the vent off and close the trunk lid. If there is no change in the voltage your light is obviously staying on! Hopefully it is something like this and not some short somewhere in the system, eek! Good luck!||||reg||2
